---
const steps = [
  { name: "Re-run the evidence in a fresh environment", body: "Never trust the author's test results." },
  { name: "Compare intent to effect", body: "Does the semantic report match what the agent said it did?" },
  { name: "Check contracts and rules from the map", body: "Declared promises and boundaries, not opinions." },
  { name: "Check that tests weren't weakened", body: "Deleted assertions, skipped tests, code that special-cases test inputs." },
  { name: "Run held-out checks the author never saw", body: "Especially tests that combine features, where failures tend to hide. The map knows which components interact." },
  { name: "Only then, judge taste", body: "Naming, structure, fit with the codebase. These count, but less." },
];
---

<section class="section wrap judge" aria-labelledby="judge-title">
  <h2 class="section-title" id="judge-title">Agents apply. A judge verifies.</h2>

  <div class="judge-grid">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Agents don't push changes. They submit applications: the stated intent, the semantic change report,
        the evidence, the scope they used and any escalations they were granted, and which agent produced it
        with which model and configuration.
      </p>
      <p>
        A second model reading the code and saying yes or no is a weak judge. Models rate their own outputs
        higher than outputs people judge equally good,<a class="cite" href="#source-7">7</a> and agents
        under pressure will edit tests or overload operators to go green.<a class="cite" href="#source-8">8</a>{" "}
        So the judge mostly re-derives facts instead of giving opinions. It sees the contracts, the map and fresh
        evidence, but not the author's reasoning, and it can run on a different vendor's model than the author.
      </p>
      <p>
        It can approve, reject with structured reasons the agent can act on, or escalate to a person. Hidden
        checks, rotating judge configurations and random human audits keep agents from simply learning to
        pass the judge.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ol class="steps">
      {
        steps.map((s, i) => (
          <li class={i === steps.length - 1 ? "is-taste" : ""}>
            <span class="s-n" aria-hidden="true">{i + 1}</span>
            <div>
              <p class="s-name">{s.name}</p>
              <p class="s-body">{s.body}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .judge-grid {
    margin-top: 2.25rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 2.5rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 62rem) {
    .judge-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 4rem;
      align-items: start;
    }
  }

  .steps {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    counter-reset: none;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  .steps li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 2.5rem 1fr;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    background: var(--paper);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-paper);
    border-left: 8px solid var(--cobalt);
    border-radius: var(--radius-strip);
    padding: 0.85rem 1rem 0.85rem 0.75rem;
  }

  .steps li.is-taste {
    background: transparent;
    border: 1px dashed var(--rule);
    border-left: 8px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .s-n {
    font-size: 1.6rem;
    font-stretch: 120%;
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1;
  }

  .s-name {
    font-weight: 650;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .s-body {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    margin-top: 0.15rem;
  }
</style>
